            "title": "Edge cases and limits",
            "problem": "Mistakes usually hide at the limits: between 19 and 20, between 49 and 50. In robot control, this difference is the difference between crashing and not crashing.",
            "contentMd": "This activity runs entirely in the **AI Workshop**, in your browser. No external service, account or key is needed. As the **Goals** list on the right turns green, you can see your progress.\n\n### Edge cases\nIf a rule says \"stop if it is less than 20\", what happens at exactly 20 is an edge case. Edge cases are the most valuable part of a test set.\n\n### Open the tool\nIn the **Eval table** tool, the system being tested this time is a robot's obstacle decision. The input has the form `distance=<cm> hız=<cm/s>` (distance, speed); the output is \"stop\", \"slow down\" or \"continue\".\n1. Write at least six cases.\n2. Try both sides of the thresholds: distance 19 and 20, 49 and 50.\n3. Do not repeat the same case.\n4. Find a case where the system gets it wrong. Hint: what should happen if the distance is medium and the speed is very high?\n\n### How does it work?\n- The rule looks at the distance; it does not take the speed into account. This is a **deliberate gap**: you will find it by testing.\n- You decide the expected label: \"for `distance=40 hız=100` (distance 40, speed 100), what do you think the system should do?\" The test compares this expectation with the system.\n- If you also use the test set while tuning the system (if you fix the rule to suit the same cases), the measurement comes out too optimistic. This is **data leakage**. Keeping one set for tuning and a separate set for the final measurement is good practice.\n\n### Test yourself\n1. If the threshold is 20 and the rule is \"stop if it is less than 20\", what does the system do for distance=20?\n2. Why does data leakage make the result look better than it is?",
            "question": "If the person who wrote this rule on a real robot had forgotten the speed, which test would have caught it in advance? What other variables would you add to the test set?",
            "explainerMd": "### What you will learn in this lesson\n- The ideas of edge cases and threshold values\n- How to test the limits of a rule\n- How to catch an input that is not taken into account with paired cases\n- What data leakage is and how it is prevented\n\n### What you should know first\nLessons 16-18: evals, writing cases, accuracy and recall. You also need to know the difference between \"less than\" (<) and \"less than or equal to\" (<=).\n\n### Mistakes hide at the limits\nThink of a rule like this for a robot: if the distance is less than 20 cm, \"stop\"; if it is less than 50 cm, \"slow down\"; otherwise, \"continue\". The numbers 20 and 50, where the rule changes its decision, are called **thresholds**. The inputs right next to a threshold are called **edge cases**: 19, 20, 49, 50. When you write code, it is very easy to write `<=` instead of `<`, and this mistake only appears at exactly the threshold value. Someone who tries 5 cm or 35 cm never sees it.\n\n### The exact behaviour of the rule\nFor this rule, the decisions for whole-number distances from 0 to 60 are:\n\n```\n0 ... 19    -> stop        (20 values)\n20 ... 49   -> slow down   (30 values)\n50 ... 60   -> continue    (11 values)\n```\n\n\"Shifting\" a threshold towards one side changes the rule: a rule that says \"stop\" at 20 cm does not meet the specification \"stop if it is less than 20\". That is why you write both sides of the threshold as cases.\n\n### An input that is not taken into account\nThis week's robot rule also sees the speed in its input, but it looks only at the distance. This is a deliberate gap: if the distance is 40 cm and the robot is going at 100 cm per second, \"slow down\" may not be enough. How do you notice this? By writing **paired cases**: two cases where only the speed changes (`distance=35 hız=10` and `distance=35 hız=90`, distance 35 with speed 10 and with speed 90). As a person you expect different answers for these two; if the rule gives the same answer, you prove that the speed is not taken into account. If you change more than one input at a time, you cannot tell which one had the effect.\n\n### Data leakage\nIf, while improving the rule, you always look at the same 6 cases and then measure success with the same 6 cases, the result may come out at 100%. That is because you tuned the rule to those cases. This is called **data leakage**, and it makes the measurement look better than it really is. The solution: keep one set for tuning and a separate set for the final measurement.\n\n### Summary\nMistakes hide right on both sides of the thresholds; that is why edge cases are the most valuable part of a test set. Inputs that are not taken into account are caught with paired cases in which only that input changes. Keeping the tuning set and the measurement set apart stops the result from coming out too optimistic.",
            "concepts": [
              {
                "term": "Edge case",
                "definition": "An input right next to the limit where the rule changes its decision.",
                "example": "If the threshold is 20, the distances 19 and 20 are edge cases.",
                "pitfall": "Trying only the \"comfortable\" values in the middle; mistakes hide at the limit."
              },
              {
                "term": "Threshold value",
                "definition": "The number at which the output switches from one label to another.",
                "example": "In the robot rule, it switches from \"stop\" to \"slow down\" at 20 cm, and from \"slow down\" to \"continue\" at 50 cm.",
                "pitfall": "Mixing up the phrases \"less than 20\" and \"20 or below\"."
              },
              {
                "term": "Missing input (speed)",
                "definition": "A variable the system does not take into account but that should affect the decision.",
                "example": "If the distance is 40 cm and the speed is 100 cm/s, this rule still says \"slow down\".",
                "pitfall": "Assuming the rule uses every input; only paired cases show this."
              },
              {
                "term": "Data leakage",
                "definition": "Also using the test cases while tuning the system, which makes the measurement look better than it is.",
                "example": "If you fix the rule to suit 6 cases and measure with the same 6 cases, it comes out at 100%.",
                "pitfall": "Doing the tuning and the measurement on the same set; the tuning set and the final measurement set should be separate."
              },
              {
                "term": "Paired (comparison) cases",
                "definition": "Two cases in which only one input changes; they test the effect of that input.",
                "example": "(`distance=35 hız=10`) and (`distance=35 hız=90`) show the effect of the speed on its own.",
                "pitfall": "Changing more than one input at a time; then it is not clear which is the cause."
              }
            ],
